WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.861
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:09.199
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:18.107
Unit twenty-one: Philosophy of Mind. In this unit, you will explore questions about the mind, choice and identity.

4
00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:20.025
free will.

5
00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:26.103
free will means the ability to make choices that are not determined by outside causes.

6
00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:30.028
Do we really have free will, or are our choices predictable?

7
00:00:30.728 --> 00:00:33.322
Your turn. Say: free will.

8
00:00:36.022 --> 00:00:36.977
free will.

9
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:39.124
determinism.

10
00:00:39.824 --> 00:00:44.273
determinism means the belief that every event is caused by earlier events.

11
00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:48.813
Determinism suggests that our decisions are already fixed.

12
00:00:49.513 --> 00:00:52.293
Your turn. Say: determinism.

13
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:56.099
determinism.

14
00:00:57.083 --> 00:00:59.118
mind-body problem.

15
00:00:59.818 --> 00:01:04.232
mind-body problem means the question of how the mind relates to the physical brain.

16
00:01:04.532 --> 00:01:08.598
The mind-body problem has puzzled philosophers for centuries.

17
00:01:09.298 --> 00:01:13.216
Your turn. Say: mind-body problem.

18
00:01:15.916 --> 00:01:17.544
mind-body problem.

19
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.445
subjective experience.

20
00:01:21.145 --> 00:01:25.258
subjective experience means what it feels like from the inside to be you.

21
00:01:25.558 --> 00:01:28.811
No scan can fully capture subjective experience.

22
00:01:29.511 --> 00:01:33.185
Your turn. Say: subjective experience.

23
00:01:35.885 --> 00:01:37.676
subjective experience.

24
00:01:38.683 --> 00:01:39.928
rationality.

25
00:01:40.628 --> 00:01:45.414
rationality means the quality of thinking based on reason and logic.

26
00:01:45.714 --> 00:01:48.666
Economists once assumed perfect rationality.

27
00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:52.541
Your turn. Say: rationality.

28
00:01:55.241 --> 00:01:56.393
rationality.

29
00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.907
personal identity.

30
00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:03.476
personal identity means what makes you the same person over time.

31
00:02:03.776 --> 00:02:06.913
Memory plays a key role in personal identity.

32
00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:10.648
Your turn. Say: personal identity.

33
00:02:13.348 --> 00:02:14.826
personal identity.

34
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:17.468
moral responsibility.

35
00:02:18.168 --> 00:02:22.803
moral responsibility means being rightly praised or blamed for your actions.

36
00:02:23.103 --> 00:02:26.391
Moral responsibility seems to require free will.

37
00:02:27.091 --> 00:02:30.730
Your turn. Say: moral responsibility.

38
00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.116
moral responsibility.

39
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:36.978
qualia.

40
00:02:37.678 --> 00:02:43.498
qualia means the individual qualities of experiences, such as the redness of red.

41
00:02:43.798 --> 00:02:47.841
Philosophers debate whether qualia can be explained by science.

42
00:02:48.541 --> 00:02:50.833
Your turn. Say: qualia.

43
00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:54.337
qualia.

44
00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.640
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.076
Ms. Sarah, I'd been reading about free will all week when I started to doubt it.

46
00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:04.744
What made you doubt it?

47
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:12.162
An article on determinism. It claimed that every decision had already been caused by brain activity.

48
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:20.259
Some experiments do suggest that. But were we really just machines, would moral responsibility make sense?

49
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.911
That's what worries me. Punishment would seem unfair.

50
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:31.560
Then there's the mind-body problem: how does the brain produce subjective experience at all?

51
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.417
Like the taste of mango or the colour of the sunset?

52
00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:43.388
Exactly. Philosophers call those qualia, and science still struggles to explain them.

53
00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:48.123
Grammar focus: Narrative tenses in reflection.

54
00:03:48.423 --> 00:03:51.862
Narrative tenses can describe how our thinking developed.

55
00:03:52.162 --> 00:03:55.868
I had been reading about free will when I started to doubt it.

56
00:03:56.168 --> 00:04:01.488
The decision had already been made, and I was about to disagree, but I stopped.

57
00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:06.980
Combining these forms lets you narrate thinking processes precisely in essays and discussions.

58
00:04:07.983 --> 00:04:10.496
Now read and listen. Are We Free?.

59
00:04:10.796 --> 00:04:14.979
For thousands of years, people have assumed that they make free choices.

60
00:04:16.283 --> 00:04:21.116
In the twentieth century, however, neuroscientists began to challenge this assumption.

61
00:04:22.417 --> 00:04:28.201
In famous experiments, brain activity appeared to start before people were aware of deciding.

62
00:04:29.517 --> 00:04:34.268
Some concluded that determinism was true and that free will was an illusion.

63
00:04:35.583 --> 00:04:41.275
Others argued that the experiments measured only simple actions, not meaningful decisions.

64
00:04:42.583 --> 00:04:48.205
The debate matters because moral responsibility seems to depend on our ability to choose.

65
00:04:49.517 --> 00:04:56.381
Meanwhile, the mind-body problem remains unsolved: no theory fully explains subjective experience.

66
00:04:57.683 --> 00:05:03.758
Perhaps the most honest conclusion is that the mind is still one of science's greatest mysteries.

67
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.621
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:07.921 --> 00:05:11.767
The belief that every event is caused by earlier events is blank.

69
00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:16.974
A, free will. B, determinism. C, qualia.

70
00:05:20.983 --> 00:05:23.412
The answer is B: determinism.

71
00:05:24.917 --> 00:05:27.973
I blank for hours when the answer suddenly came to me.

72
00:05:28.273 --> 00:05:32.193
A, had been thinking. B, have thought. C, was think.

73
00:05:36.183 --> 00:05:38.972
The answer is A: had been thinking.

74
00:05:40.483 --> 00:05:43.760
By the time I arrived, they blank the decision.

75
00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:49.234
A, already made. B, had already made. C, have already made.

76
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.132
The answer is B: had already made.

77
00:05:57.617 --> 00:06:00.429
What makes you the same person over time is blank.

78
00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:08.051
A, personal identity. B, rationality. C, subjective experience.

79
00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:15.396
The answer is A: personal identity.

80
00:06:16.883 --> 00:06:19.916
According to the text, why does the debate matter?

81
00:06:20.216 --> 00:06:27.329
A, for economics. B, because moral responsibility seems to depend on choice. C, for sport.

82
00:06:31.317 --> 00:06:37.008
The answer is B: because moral responsibility seems to depend on choice.

83
00:06:38.517 --> 00:06:42.934
Now it's your turn. Part one: Do you believe humans have free will?

84
00:06:48.234 --> 00:06:54.436
Part two: Give your position, two reasons, and explain what it means for responsibility.

85
00:07:00.736 --> 00:07:13.341
I believe we have a limited form of free will. Our genes and environment shape us, but we can still reflect and change our habits. That's why people should be held responsible, but also given chances to change.

86
00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:16.116
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:17.117 --> 00:07:22.219
Great work today! You learned eight new words and narrative tenses in reflection.

88
00:07:22.519 --> 00:07:26.773
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:27.073 --> 00:07:29.691
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:29.991 --> 00:07:32.864
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

